WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.042 --> 00:00:05.219
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.219 --> 00:00:09.330
It's a pleasure sitting across from you on
stage after interning at

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.087
the Gates Foundation this past summer.

00:00:12.087 --> 00:00:16.604
So I want to spend some time today talking
a little bit about the values and

00:00:16.604 --> 00:00:20.450
decisions that have really shaped where
you are today.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:24.550
As well as hear a, a little bit of your
thoughts around where you are going.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:27.190
As well as where the Gates Foundation is
going.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:28.740
&gt;&gt; Sounds good, I'm glad to be here.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:29.870
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:34.740
So let's start off by talking about two of
the really big pillars that

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.190
have been constant in your career,
healthcare and education.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:44.040
And in fact, your father was a pharmacist
and your mother was a teacher.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.976
So, I'm interested to hear a little bit
about how,

00:00:46.976 --> 00:00:49.196
what kinds of lessons they taught you.

00:00:49.196 --> 00:00:54.410
And how have those lessons really shaped,
the way you've thought about your career?

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:58.210
&gt;&gt; Oh, as you all just heard, I grew up in
Reno,

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.920
Nevada, biggest little city in the world.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.420
And although I'm a California native.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:04.590
So now that I'm in Seattle by

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:07.588
the way I'm getting all sorts of grief
about Kaepernick.

00:01:07.588 --> 00:01:11.340
[LAUGH] lot of trash talk up there.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.020
But, but, but I, I feel like today and

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:21.350
my entire career it has been so heavily
shaped by how I was raised.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.170
I'm one of seven kids.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:30.100
And, number two of seven, and from a very
early age there was-

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:31.830
&gt;&gt; There were a couple of things that,

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:35.150
that I got from my parents that stay with
me today.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:39.210
One is a fierce love of reading and

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.549
learning, from my mom, who was an English
teacher.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.360
and, and a sense of the privilege it is to
be involved in health,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.690
from my dad, who's a pharmacist.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:53.030
And being able to work in my dad's
drugstore when I was young, and

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:59.420
seeing how my dad was so focused on people
who were suffering or sick.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:03.910
And trying to make them better, made a big
impact on my wish to

00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:06.340
see if I could do something to contribute
in medicine.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:13.050
&gt;&gt; And you've made a lot of really
unorthodox decisions in your career.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:17.880
First going to Uganda in the late 1980s to
study HIV/AIDS at

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.190
a time where not very much was known about
the disease.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:24.520
And later, leaving the number one
pharmaceutical company to

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:30.350
go to what was a relatively new and
struggling biotech company, Genentech.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:33.800
Not a lot of people would take those kinds
of risks.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:38.290
So tell me a little bit about what you
were looking for at those times.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:42.559
How were you weighing the benefits versus
the costs of those decisions?

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.850
&gt;&gt; Yeah I think it's, it, actually learned
a lot of lessons both about leadership and

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:52.890
about risk taking from both of those,
opportunities.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:54.868
So I had been at, at UCSF for

00:02:54.868 --> 00:02:59.512
seven years when the Rockefeller
Foundation came to UCSF.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:04.220
And asked UCSF to study heterosexual
transmission of HIV.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:07.110
It's hard to remember now, but at the
time.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:10.600
In 1988 it AIDS was thought to

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:15.770
be a disease related to either being gay
or having a blood product.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:20.300
And it, there was a myth then

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:25.230
that in fact there was this secret
homosexuality going on in Africa.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.100
And that must have been why.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.940
That people were too embarrassed to
confess that they were gay.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.800
But that, that heterosexual transmission
of HIV was probably a myth.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.930
And so, when Rockefeller came to UCSF.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:42.250
UCSF asked me and my then husband,

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:48.170
who's an infectious disease doctor, to
move to Kampala to work in Uganda.

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:53.960
And, and I have a very distinctive memory
of one of my dear colleagues, Bob Cohen.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:58.660
Telling me that I would ruin my apa,
academic career by moving to Uganda.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.042
It was really good advice.

00:04:01.042 --> 00:04:06.838
And in fact [LAUGH] it actually it, it, it
was good advice because he was right.

00:04:06.838 --> 00:04:09.254
For two years, I didn't publish.

00:04:09.254 --> 00:04:10.977
Uh,uh, I didn't schmooze or

00:04:10.977 --> 00:04:15.000
interact or do things you do to become a
tenure-track faculty member.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.692
And when we came back from Uganda which
was

00:04:18.692 --> 00:04:22.962
a completely life changing experience in
every way.

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:24.913
There, there were two things stayed with
me.

00:04:24.913 --> 00:04:27.839
One is that my academic career was over.

00:04:27.839 --> 00:04:32.640
And I needed to leave and figure out how
to pay the rent, and, amd make a living.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.130
And two, that I would never be the same.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:38.990
And that my own expectations of myself.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.850
All the privilege I had had my whole life.

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:44.520
And what I expected from myself as a
leader.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.730
And as somebody who, who was so fortunate
to have education and

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:52.750
clean water and great parents and a great
family and a wonderful husband.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:55.380
That I needed to give back.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.620
And I needed to do something.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.709
I will tell you when I became chancellor
at UCSF I called up Bob.

00:05:01.709 --> 00:05:05.385
[LAUGH] And I said hey Bob I made it back.

00:05:05.385 --> 00:05:07.078
[LAUGH] So I can work down all right.

00:05:07.078 --> 00:05:12.040
[LAUGH] I just skipped that whole
associate professor thing.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:17.460
[LAUGH] So, so that was that was a huge
risk it,

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.030
it especially to my burgeoning academic
career.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:25.190
But a really good risk.

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:30.240
So that later when I was at Bristol-Myers
Squibb and I was working on Taxol.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:36.430
Which was their number one cancer drug and
a terrific, new advance in chemotherapy.

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:42.780
I visited Genentech and that was in, in
95.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.920
And Art Levinson was the head of R and

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:49.510
D and Art talked to me about Genentech's
wish to be a cancer company.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:52.710
But Genentech had no marketed products
that were cancer drugs.

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:58.890
But they had terrific science an
incredible vision that Art had that they

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:00.870
would lead in cancer.

00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:05.000
And that Genetech would transform how
people were treated who had cancer.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:09.970
And I was so deeply inspired by that
vision that I thought,

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:15.560
well, you know, nothing may come of this,
but I want to be part of that.

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:20.020
And having had the experience of taking a
risk before.

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:24.080
And knowing that the consequences of not
taking that risk and

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.595
not seeing what might be possible wasn't
acceptable to me.

00:06:27.595 --> 00:06:36.110
And, and so that, that career risk taking
seemed, seemed totally reasonable.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:40.370
Which is probably why I'm not at UCSF
anymore, I'm at the Gates Foundation.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:43.395
&gt;&gt; And you mentioned Art Levinson.

00:06:43.395 --> 00:06:44.150
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:06:44.150 --> 00:06:45.940
&gt;&gt; Who hired you.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:50.440
Really helped you catapult your career as
an executive at Genentech.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:53.970
Art has said that when you first got
there, you were a little bit soft.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:57.160
You needed to learn how to make tough
decisions.

00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:58.289
Do you agree with that assessment?

00:06:58.289 --> 00:06:59.850
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And if so,

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:02.230
how did you develop that ability.

00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:04.300
It depends on your definition of soft.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:05.341
&gt;&gt; Sure.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:05.341 --> 00:07:09.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So Ar, Ar, Art was my boss for

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.670
most of the 14 years I was at Genentech
and

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:15.820
we remained really close friends and
colleagues.

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:18.660
Which I think is, is one of

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:24.140
those special things that I hope that all
of you in your careers get to experience.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:28.736
The combination of a boss who is so
inspiring and so

00:07:28.736 --> 00:07:34.215
driven and passionate about having a
successful company.

00:07:34.215 --> 00:07:37.720
And making a difference for patients.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.602
And sometimes, so deeply annoying.

00:07:40.602 --> 00:07:41.296
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:07:41.296 --> 00:07:42.810
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And, and

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:48.280
there were two things annoying about Art
for me that I grew to be really fond of.

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:50.380
One was him pushing me.

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:55.150
That I should be tough on, on everything.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:59.620
That, that as driven as I am and as I was.

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:03.280
It was okay to be demanding others in
service of what we

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:04.440
were trying to accomplish.

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:06.704
That I shouldn't be embarrassed about
that, or

00:08:06.704 --> 00:08:09.653
think that people would think that I'm, or
too tough.

00:08:09.653 --> 00:08:14.024
And so I do think there was sorta a
toughness factor, and

00:08:14.024 --> 00:08:18.210
not being afraid of asking people to
overachieve.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:21.070
In service in what we were trying to do
that I learn from Art.

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:22.860
But the other thing,

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:27.020
and a really special thing that I push
myself to think about all the time is.

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:33.190
I got from Art that he really felt like I
consistently underestimated myself.

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:37.730
And in, in the best of ways.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:41.560
He would demonstrate to me that he was
gonna keep raising the bar for

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:45.090
what he wanted from me or what he expected
from me.

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:48.280
And yet, he was completely confident I was
up to it.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:50.110
Even when I wasn't.

00:08:50.110 --> 00:08:54.800
And, as a leader and as somebody who
mentors or

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.400
manages other people, that sense that.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:04.170
It, it isn't being nice to them to
underestimate them or be easy on, on them.

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:08.840
But to say I'll bet you're capable of more
and, and yeah, I,

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:12.190
I'm gonna ask you hard things, but you're
up to it.

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.970
That was a really special thing to have a
boss like that.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:16.109
I'm very grateful for that.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:20.848
&gt;&gt; What is, what do you think it's like to
work for you at this point?

00:09:20.848 --> 00:09:23.379
So, Art was a tremendous influence.

00:09:23.379 --> 00:09:24.748
No, I'm interested to know.

00:09:24.748 --> 00:09:29.228
I think you, how would you, how do you
think the people who work for

00:09:29.228 --> 00:09:31.388
you describe your leadership.

00:09:31.388 --> 00:09:35.782
&gt;&gt; Gosh, I should, can I make up something
really great?

00:09:35.782 --> 00:09:38.920
[LAUGH] And hope they'll live up to that.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:41.540
Well, I'll tell you what I hope they
would, they would feel.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:46.828
When I get excited about things, I'm all
in, you know.

00:09:46.828 --> 00:09:47.736
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:09:47.736 --> 00:09:53.610
&gt;&gt; And I'm I've been so incredibly lucky
to be involved with things

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:58.440
from trying to change the way cancer's
treated to creating a great university.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:03.210
To literally changing the world through
our work at the foundation.

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:09.903
I hope that what people who I work with
would say about me is I'm fierce and

00:10:09.903 --> 00:10:16.570
demanding and and sincere in my wish that
we make a big difference.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:26.690
And what I want it to be like when I'm
leading is both fun and exhausted.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:34.240
Cuz I, I honestly think it's you don't get
great things without trying hard.

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:37.940
And you know, this room's filled with
people who are overachievers.

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:38.910
I know that about you.

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:44.137
And so many overachievers feel like, oh my
god,

00:10:44.137 --> 00:10:47.400
you know, I need more work-life balance.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:50.910
Or I need to be easier on myself or I need
to get more sleep.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:56.900
But there's something really special about
being fierce when you're

00:10:56.900 --> 00:11:00.810
doing something important, or you wanna
create something special.

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:06.550
And, and, I hope that, that when I'm at my
best, I'm fierce.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:09.945
&gt;&gt; I know you've said before carpe diem
is,

00:11:09.945 --> 00:11:11.710
is something that you really believe in.

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:16.435
So it's interesting to hear how that plays
out in your leadership philosophy.

00:11:16.435 --> 00:11:19.690
Um,you see, I think one of the interesting
things about your career is

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:22.380
that you've spent time both on the
commercial sector side.

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:27.540
And in the, the not for profit education
as well as now the philanthropic sector.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:33.110
What do you think the lessons are that you
took from your time

00:11:33.110 --> 00:11:38.600
on the corporate side to UCSF when you
went there as Chancellor.

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.560
Huge recession, budget cuts across the
state,

00:11:41.560 --> 00:11:43.860
what were the biggest things that you were
thinking about.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:47.830
And how did you take your perspective,
your very unique perspective, and

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:49.270
use it to make change?

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:54.420
&gt;&gt; Well, I, I think the, the, the
experience I had in

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:57.290
the corporate sector was helpful to me in
moving to UCSF.

00:11:57.290 --> 00:11:59.790
And now to the Foundation in a couple of
ways.

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:05.800
One is, and it's something I like a lot,
about the for

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:08.580
profit publicly traded company world.

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:13.890
There's a certain orderliness, and set of
expectations that come regularly.

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:17.120
Once a quarter you have to tell the whole
world how you're doing.

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:20.970
And you have to do so with metrics that
are widely understood.

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:24.730
Earnings per share, sales.

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:26.070
If, if you're in product development,

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:29.720
number of approvals, product launches, do
people like your product?

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:31.530
Are they using them?

00:12:31.530 --> 00:12:37.270
And I think particularly when you're in
science and when you're in the R&amp;D world,

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:42.330
it can be easy to continue to push the
finish line forward.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:45.860
And, and to, to put off accountability.

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:51.470
And so, I think first and foremost the
commercial world

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:56.750
resonated for me in a very high
accountability, metrics-orientated way.

00:12:56.750 --> 00:13:00.681
And so bringing that model of high
accountability and using metrics to,

00:13:00.681 --> 00:13:05.412
to the academic world was important to me
and served me well.

00:13:05.412 --> 00:13:11.333
The other aspect of, of the private world
that that I brought to use at UCSF,

00:13:11.333 --> 00:13:13.610
and already was at use at UCSF.

00:13:13.610 --> 00:13:17.355
But I probably expanded is the wish to
have effective public,

00:13:17.355 --> 00:13:21.110
private partnerships, and to be realistic
about them.

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:23.170
And I, I'll give you an example.

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:27.060
I, I think one of the most efficient ways
for people to lead.

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:31.280
Is to be really clear about what you're
asking of people and

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:32.960
what the outcomes are.

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:37.624
Too often in academia we would be
approaching private companies and

00:13:37.624 --> 00:13:41.993
somehow, out of fear of rejection, I
think, we would be unclear.

00:13:41.993 --> 00:13:44.180
We would be asking for charity.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:46.930
Like, could you write us a check because
you care about UCSF and

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:49.320
we're going through a tough time?

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:50.940
But we would pretend it's a business deal.

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:55.970
And I would often talk to faculty members
about these, these public

00:13:55.970 --> 00:14:01.000
private partnerships and push ourselves
first, but then the companies as well.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:02.910
Is this a part of your business plan.

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:05.990
Are we part of your business plan cuz
that's called a deal?

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:06.830
What do you need?

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:07.710
What do we need?

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:09.300
What, what money exchanges hands?

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:11.280
What are the expectations?

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:15.780
Or do you have a charitable part of what
you're doing that you'd like to

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:19.079
give us a check and we'll thank you and,
and be grateful.

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:22.890
Those are really different things.

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:26.770
And the consequences of misunderstanding
the difference between a deal and

00:14:26.770 --> 00:14:28.780
charity are high.

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:31.920
And so, partly because I had come from the
private sector.

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:34.045
Understanding what a head of R and D or

00:14:34.045 --> 00:14:37.072
a head of a company is looking for for
their company.

00:14:37.072 --> 00:14:39.448
And what UCSF needed and was looking for

00:14:39.448 --> 00:14:43.840
for us as a public institution, hopefully
there'd be some marriage.

00:14:43.840 --> 00:14:46.339
But if there wasn't, it was better not to
even start.

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:53.086
&gt;&gt; One of the more controversial proposals
that you had at UCSF.

00:14:53.086 --> 00:14:59.016
Was potentially separating some of the
operations and

00:14:59.016 --> 00:15:03.767
finances of the UCSF campus from the UC
system.

00:15:03.767 --> 00:15:07.526
That didn't end up coming to fruition and
I'm curious to know,

00:15:07.526 --> 00:15:11.912
is there anything that you learned from
putting that proposal out there and

00:15:11.912 --> 00:15:15.949
having it in some senses fail in the way
that you didn't intend,

00:15:15.949 --> 00:15:18.969
what could you have done differently if
anything?

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:25.900
I think that, that I learned a lot of
lessons for, from that one is I

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:32.250
think it's eh, in service of the
university being a great institution.

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:33.760
I I'll tell you what I feel good about.

00:15:33.760 --> 00:15:37.650
I feel good about pushing, pushing really
hard that anything I

00:15:37.650 --> 00:15:39.870
could do to serve the governance of UCSF
in a positive way, and

00:15:39.870 --> 00:15:45.090
leverage all the assets that UCSF had and
has in service of being the best

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:48.920
institution we could possibly be for
California and for the world.

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:55.910
Is something I was very motivated by and
driven by,

00:15:55.910 --> 00:16:00.840
in my quest to increase the independence
of UCSF on the governance front.

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:07.310
Where I think I probably, underestimated
is the passion with which the Regent's

00:16:07.310 --> 00:16:13.160
look at the whole of the ten universities
of California as, as one.

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:15.898
And although I thought it was pretty low
risk for

00:16:15.898 --> 00:16:20.363
them to take a graduate student only life
sciences, only, am I selling you?

00:16:20.363 --> 00:16:21.106
[LAUGH].

00:16:21.106 --> 00:16:22.917
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:16:22.917 --> 00:16:27.401
&gt;&gt; As an experiment, one-time only, low
consequence, [LAUGH] I,

00:16:27.401 --> 00:16:29.837
I think I underestimated two things.

00:16:29.837 --> 00:16:31.207
One is the history and

00:16:31.207 --> 00:16:36.539
the passion with which people care about
the unified UC system, and the other thing

00:16:36.539 --> 00:16:41.586
is how quickly my colleagues, especially
at Berkeley, wanted to go with me.

00:16:41.586 --> 00:16:42.376
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:16:42.376 --> 00:16:45.833
&gt;&gt; And so me positioning this as a
one-time experiment,

00:16:45.833 --> 00:16:50.492
only UCSF as Berkeley was kind of latching
on and, and coming along,

00:16:50.492 --> 00:16:55.803
it kind of made it seem like a sham that
this was a one-time small experiments.

00:16:55.803 --> 00:17:01.203
and, and so I learned a lot from that, and
I also learned about the importance

00:17:01.203 --> 00:17:05.853
of not going so fast that you kinda hit a
wall but you know,

00:17:05.853 --> 00:17:12.050
at the Gates Foundation, we have this
saying that we're impatient optimists.

00:17:12.050 --> 00:17:17.207
My impatience may have gotten the best of
me [LAUGH] cuz I wanted it now, and and

00:17:17.207 --> 00:17:19.787
my optimism probably, got the best of me,

00:17:19.787 --> 00:17:23.020
too, in thinking that people would go
along.

00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:29.000
On the other hand my successor Chancellor
Hawgood continues to reap

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:35.440
the benefits of the Regent's understanding
better the business model of UCSF.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:41.250
How UCSF operates, how an academic medial
center receives funds and

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:45.190
the importance of being nimble in today's
world of healthcare?

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:49.794
And so I hope that the institution and,
and Chancellor Hawgood continue to

00:17:49.794 --> 00:17:53.754
reap the benefits of leaning out maybe a
little ahead of my skis.

00:17:53.754 --> 00:17:55.380
[LAUGH].

00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:59.270
&gt;&gt; As a, as a Biotech Executive, eh,
really responsible for

00:17:59.270 --> 00:18:01.770
the R&amp;D side of things.

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:07.120
You really have to become very comfortable
with the risk of failure very early on,

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:11.170
and think about the portfolio, and how
you're gonna manage that appropriately.

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:16.020
How do you as a leader think about
treating failure and taking risks, and

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:20.629
how do you sort of, let that viewpoint
trickle down within your organization?

00:18:23.070 --> 00:18:28.410
&gt;&gt; The risk of failure to me is something
that is a part of being an innovator,

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:32.925
I don't think you can do excellent
innovation without-

00:18:32.925 --> 00:18:34.140
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]
&gt;&gt; Failing.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:34.874
And, and-

00:18:34.874 --> 00:18:38.250
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]
&gt;&gt; Failing serially sometimes, but, but

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:41.810
I think that there's, there's a couple of
ways that I thought about that and

00:18:41.810 --> 00:18:43.590
I continue to think about that.

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:48.900
Think of the key part about leadership to
have an environment that accepts failure,

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:53.050
and welcomes failure and encourages people
to take risks.

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:58.690
But I mentioned before how, eh, how, how
accountability's really important to me,

00:18:58.690 --> 00:19:02.140
and so one of things I think in, in any
venture when you

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:06.610
have a portfolio of projects, and I had a
portfolio of projects at Genentech and

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:10.710
I had a portfolio of projects at the
foundation that we oversee.

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:13.440
No institution fail serially and

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:18.870
succeed, and so what, what I would always
ask of people,

00:19:18.870 --> 00:19:23.390
and, and especially in the clinical trials
world, so that pertains to the,

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:27.580
the things we're doing in global health,
but, but even more broadly.

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:34.300
Our job as innovators is to seek truth,
and if you're making a new product for

00:19:34.300 --> 00:19:38.479
human beings, truth might be the that
product is neither safe, nor effective.

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:42.762
What the company needed us, and actually
what the world-

00:19:42.762 --> 00:19:43.604
&gt;&gt; [COUGH]

00:19:43.604 --> 00:19:46.883
&gt;&gt; Needs now, is clear answers that we
believe.

00:19:46.883 --> 00:19:52.430
Sometimes the answer is no, and so the job
of people who are innovators

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:57.400
is to quickly and efficiently get an
answer that we believe, and so

00:19:57.400 --> 00:20:03.970
I try to set an environment where I
rewarded, clear, efficient answers.

00:20:03.970 --> 00:20:07.850
And I particularly awa, awarded in face of
human health,

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:11.320
if the answer is this is unsafe, this
isn't good for patients or

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:14.465
there's a better drug that somebody else
has and it's not gonna be us.

00:20:14.465 --> 00:20:15.913
Eh, if you reward and

00:20:15.913 --> 00:20:20.590
celebrate the people, who get to that
answer, good things happen.

00:20:20.590 --> 00:20:24.400
If you, if you expect the answer is always
yes or

00:20:24.400 --> 00:20:30.807
that people have projects that always
succeed, you start playing small ball?

00:20:30.807 --> 00:20:34.650
You have small incremental progress, if
any progress at all and

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:36.937
if people find an answer that's a no?

00:20:36.937 --> 00:20:40.531
They don't wanna tell you that and so part
of,

00:20:40.531 --> 00:20:45.601
the most important part of leading an
innovative environment for

00:20:45.601 --> 00:20:50.410
me is enabling people to seek the truth
and tell you the truth.

00:20:52.140 --> 00:20:56.010
&gt;&gt; I know you've had a chance to think a
lot about seeking truth and

00:20:56.010 --> 00:21:00.790
how to create that kind of culture within
an organization at the Gates Foundation

00:21:00.790 --> 00:21:02.800
which you joined last May.

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:07.960
So it's been not quite a year yet, but a
lot has happened since then and

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:11.150
I'm curious, you know, now that you've had
some time under your belt,

00:21:11.150 --> 00:21:15.070
what do you want your personal legacy at
the Gates Foundation to be as it

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:18.550
heads into its you know, upcoming 15th
year.

00:21:18.550 --> 00:21:20.680
And I believe it's in its 15th year, right
now.

00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:22.886
So what do you want your legacy to be
there?

00:21:22.886 --> 00:21:27.190
&gt;&gt; Well, eh, eh, it, there's a few things
that I care about most,

00:21:27.190 --> 00:21:31.410
Some of them are short term, but you're
asking about legacy, eh,

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:36.760
it's not uncommon for people to remind me
that I am the CEO

00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:43.586
of the largest foundation in the world,
and that's interesting.

00:21:43.586 --> 00:21:46.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] big is good.

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:50.950
We have scale, we can make important
things happen, so in the, in, in

00:21:50.950 --> 00:21:57.600
the simplest of ways I'd rather be known,
as the best foundation in the world.

00:21:57.600 --> 00:21:58.820
And the foundation that,

00:21:58.820 --> 00:22:03.380
that made a really big difference, that
made important things happen.

00:22:03.380 --> 00:22:08.180
And, so what that means for me, it kinda
breaks down into if I

00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:13.670
want us to move from biggest to really
that's, that's

00:22:13.670 --> 00:22:19.000
the foundation that if I have wealth,
makes me wanna be a philanthropist.

00:22:19.000 --> 00:22:22.285
If I can do a job that makes me wanna work
in that not for

00:22:22.285 --> 00:22:27.139
profit sector, eh, eh, if I wanna vote a
certain way is it's to vote for

00:22:27.139 --> 00:22:31.140
the kind of policies that enable the work
the foundation does.

00:22:31.140 --> 00:22:34.925
All of that means that the work we do at
the foundation needs to

00:22:34.925 --> 00:22:39.157
take our investments, which in the bigger
world, although we're

00:22:39.157 --> 00:22:44.670
the biggest foundation are small versus
government and, and other sectors.

00:22:44.670 --> 00:22:49.725
Especially, the private sector, that, that
our work enables that transformation we

00:22:49.725 --> 00:22:54.230
wanna have happen and that, that that's
visible and meaningful.

00:22:54.230 --> 00:23:00.810
And that we attract great talent because
the culture of the foundation means that,

00:23:00.810 --> 00:23:03.440
you know, I'm, I'm on the Proctor and
Gamble board, and

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:08.840
it's really interesting to me that people
like Scott Cook and Meg Whitman still

00:23:08.840 --> 00:23:13.060
talk about that early on, their first job
was at Procter and Gamble.

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:16.590
I want people to say, you know, during my
career, I was at the Gates Foundation,

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:22.180
every bit as much as Meg says, you know, I
was a Product Manager at P&amp;G,

00:23:22.180 --> 00:23:26.010
because that means that's a special
life-changing experience for

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:29.840
that person, so I wanna lead the
organization that is that organization.

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:33.290
&gt;&gt; What do you think are gonna be the
biggest challenges for

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:38.228
you to get to that goal which having
worked at the Gates Foundation I, I,

00:23:38.228 --> 00:23:41.954
love, and definitely adhere to, what are
those challenges going to be and

00:23:41.954 --> 00:23:44.733
how are you mitigating them through your
leadership?

00:23:44.733 --> 00:23:49.541
&gt;&gt; Well, I think there's challenges on two
fronts, one is,

00:23:49.541 --> 00:23:53.370
we have audacious ambitions, you know, if,

00:23:53.370 --> 00:23:59.066
if I start running off the list it makes
me feel a little sweaty you know,

00:23:59.066 --> 00:24:04.852
[LAUGH] it just it, it's solve polio,
tackle malaria, tuberculosis,

00:24:04.852 --> 00:24:12.126
HIV [COUGH] African Sleeping Sickness im,
improve lives for small holder farmers.

00:24:12.126 --> 00:24:17.125
Get 120 million more women on modern
contraception by 2020 oh, oh, and

00:24:17.125 --> 00:24:20.270
fix the US educational system while we're
at it.

00:24:20.270 --> 00:24:21.291
&gt;&gt; Okay, [INAUDIBLE].

00:24:21.291 --> 00:24:22.505
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:24:22.505 --> 00:24:24.180
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] that too, ,.

00:24:24.180 --> 00:24:27.130
So, I, I think on the impact front,

00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:31.960
because we're tackling some of the world's
hardest problems with metrics,

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:36.340
we're really visibly pushing ourselves to
make a huge difference.

00:24:36.340 --> 00:24:42.320
And so for me, being a great foundation
starts with impact and,

00:24:42.320 --> 00:24:47.720
and that impact requires us to be great
collaborators, work with others

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:53.770
towards is very important, audacious goals
and so, doing that effectively means

00:24:53.770 --> 00:24:58.460
it starts with the, the awareness we have
of our place in the world-

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:03.520
How can our capital service leverage,
serve as lubricant, be transformational?

00:25:03.520 --> 00:25:07.373
What are the unique things we can do as a
foundation that others won't do?

00:25:07.373 --> 00:25:14.137
And on the culture side, it is hitting
that sweets spot that I mentioned before,

00:25:14.137 --> 00:25:18.142
I always felt like my best days at work
have come just in

00:25:18.142 --> 00:25:22.147
the perfect middle of feeling really
comfortable and

00:25:22.147 --> 00:25:27.418
free, like I can do anything and totally
terrified and being frozen.

00:25:27.418 --> 00:25:33.810
And, and so somewhere at the Gates
Foundation I want people to feel like,

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:38.570
man I can make anything happen but we we
have just incredible talented people,

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:41.240
who should feel that way.

00:25:41.240 --> 00:25:45.694
And it's a high wire act to pull off, what
we're trying to pull off, so

00:25:45.694 --> 00:25:50.910
can we just hit that middle, you know,
where you're both terrified and excited.

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:57.040
Making a culture for really talented
people where it feels just right,-

00:25:57.040 --> 00:26:01.143
&gt;&gt; [COUGH].
&gt;&gt; Exhilarated not overwhelming, excited,

00:26:01.143 --> 00:26:06.843
and little terrified that kind of culture
is special and wonderful,

00:26:06.843 --> 00:26:12.170
and takes enormous energy every single
day, to live up to that.

00:26:14.470 --> 00:26:18.490
&gt;&gt; You're working with two of the most
engaged and

00:26:18.490 --> 00:26:22.340
smartest philanthropists that there are in
the world, Bill and Melinda.

00:26:22.340 --> 00:26:25.220
I feel I have a responsibility to ask what
so

00:26:25.220 --> 00:26:29.080
many of the audience are, are thinking,
what's it like to work with them?

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:33.786
And in particular, how have you had to
adjust your leadership style in

00:26:33.786 --> 00:26:37.711
order to work effectively with this type
of partnership?

00:26:37.711 --> 00:26:39.290
Well, I work at a family business.

00:26:39.290 --> 00:26:39.941
[LAUGH].

00:26:39.941 --> 00:26:42.587
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:26:42.587 --> 00:26:47.696
&gt;&gt; Or as other people in the foundation
world have told me, your donors are alive.

00:26:47.696 --> 00:26:48.578
[LAUGH].

00:26:48.578 --> 00:26:49.229
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:26:49.229 --> 00:26:50.344
&gt;&gt; They're very alive I can tell you.

00:26:50.344 --> 00:26:54.802
[LAUGH] Happily, eh, eh-

00:26:54.802 --> 00:26:55.683
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:26:55.683 --> 00:27:00.282
&gt;&gt; It, you know, it's eh, I'm not sure how
to answer your question, I, I, I

00:27:00.282 --> 00:27:05.066
learned a very important leadership lesson
and, and we talked about Art Levinson.

00:27:05.066 --> 00:27:08.850
And, and eh, eh, it, one of the most
important things I learned from

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:12.810
working with Art, who came from R&amp;D to
being CEO.

00:27:13.980 --> 00:27:20.304
He did not adjust his personality to
become the CEO, [NOISE] he was as goofy

00:27:20.304 --> 00:27:27.267
and as, as nerdy as he was when he was the
head of research when he became the CEO.

00:27:27.267 --> 00:27:30.071
And so I think great leaders, and

00:27:30.071 --> 00:27:35.100
I know leaders that I admire show up as
himselves every day.

00:27:35.100 --> 00:27:39.840
And so when I approached being the CEO
with Bill and Melinda, I knew they were

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:45.285
two extraordinary special people and I
said I'm just gonna be myself.

00:27:45.285 --> 00:27:50.560
Uh,eh, it, and I'm gonna be myself through
the whole interview process, and

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:54.180
if that's embarrassing to them or if I'm
too weird or, you know, they don't like

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:56.850
how I operate, then they'll figure it out
during the interview process.

00:27:56.850 --> 00:27:58.647
And they won't be surprised.

00:27:58.647 --> 00:28:02.092
And so I had an interview with Bill that
was funny-

00:28:02.092 --> 00:28:03.959
&gt;&gt; [COUGH].
&gt;&gt; Cuz people had told me boy when he

00:28:03.959 --> 00:28:06.676
gets mad, you know, [NOISE] it's gonna be
bad, so

00:28:06.676 --> 00:28:10.131
I thought well maybe I should test what
it's like if he's mad.

00:28:10.131 --> 00:28:11.942
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:28:11.942 --> 00:28:16.110
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So, so I had read something
about a criticism about the,

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:18.620
the foundation's focus on polio
eradication, you know,

00:28:18.620 --> 00:28:20.310
there's a lot of other problems in the
world and

00:28:20.310 --> 00:28:24.080
we're spending too much and so, when I was
interviewing with Bill, I said,

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:27.260
so what do you think about all the
criticism of the polio efforts?

00:28:27.260 --> 00:28:28.900
I thought, well, maybe that'll get him,

00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:32.300
you know, cuz I wanted to test what it
would be like if he was fired up.

00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:35.720
And you know, he did react [LAUGH] and

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:40.000
then I realized there's a really big
difference between getting him fired up,

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:44.931
and being mad, and being mad at me, which
that didn't really test that well.

00:28:44.931 --> 00:28:48.986
[LAUGH] so happily I haven't had that
experience-

00:28:48.986 --> 00:28:52.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] But, but I would say,

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:57.260
I am so enjoying working with Bill and
Melinda, they're very,

00:28:57.260 --> 00:29:02.720
very different from each other, and very
complimentary, and, and

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:10.662
what they have in common is incredible,
almost impossible to explain generosity.

00:29:10.662 --> 00:29:15.305
And the, the most sincere wish that their
generosity and

00:29:15.305 --> 00:29:19.852
their foundation changes the world in
positive ways and

00:29:19.852 --> 00:29:24.512
that's real and fundamental and the reason
I'm there.

00:29:24.512 --> 00:29:30.030
And, and Bill's impatience, doggedness,
brilliance and, and

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:35.186
everything that Bill is and everything
he's brought to the world,

00:29:35.186 --> 00:29:38.895
he's bringing to the work at the
foundation is,

00:29:38.895 --> 00:29:44.890
is just magnificent you know, it's just a
really special, powerful thing.

00:29:44.890 --> 00:29:50.164
And Melinda's sense of social justice, her
incredible caring for

00:29:50.164 --> 00:29:55.992
women and girls, she's like the world's
best, most enthusiastic mom,

00:29:55.992 --> 00:30:01.469
it brings all that to the job, and so in
any given day I get to work with.

00:30:01.469 --> 00:30:06.656
Bill and Melinda, or Bill or Melinda, and
tap into all the power and passion and

00:30:06.656 --> 00:30:09.610
intellect that both of them bring to the
job.

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:13.190
And we have to get stuff done.

00:30:13.190 --> 00:30:17.146
And and that's their expectations.

00:30:17.146 --> 00:30:20.612
The other thing I'm learning, which I
think is just a really important thing.

00:30:20.612 --> 00:30:26.898
Both in a family business, but in anything
you do in life where you have co-chairs.

00:30:26.898 --> 00:30:27.900
They're not the same.

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:29.540
They don't always agree.

00:30:29.540 --> 00:30:33.457
And so one of the most important things I
can do is to point out whether coming at

00:30:33.457 --> 00:30:35.460
something from two different places.

00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:40.565
And it's part of, my background in science
and technology.

00:30:40.565 --> 00:30:43.068
I think truth comes out of messiness.

00:30:43.068 --> 00:30:45.910
And I think that's an asset I bring to the
foundation.

00:30:45.910 --> 00:30:49.100
That if Bill and Melinda disagree, or see
things in different ways,

00:30:49.100 --> 00:30:53.060
because of their different perspectives, I
wanna make that visible.

00:30:53.060 --> 00:30:54.200
I'm not afraid of that.

00:30:54.200 --> 00:30:55.840
In fact, I embrace that.

00:30:55.840 --> 00:31:00.990
Because my fundamental belief as a leader
is, the more disparate voices, for

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:04.356
me a big part of diversity and why I like
diversity.

00:31:04.356 --> 00:31:08.541
Is the more different views that come from
very different places,

00:31:08.541 --> 00:31:10.790
really good outcomes will happen.

00:31:10.790 --> 00:31:14.700
We'll get a, a better answer, or the best
answer, from that.

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:18.960
So I'm trying to tap into that part of me,
and that affinity I have for

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:23.100
differences to be a good partner for the
two of them.

00:31:24.830 --> 00:31:28.930
&gt;&gt; I think one more question before we
turn it over to the audience for Q and A.

00:31:28.930 --> 00:31:34.200
But, you have been able to balance that
delicate intersection between

00:31:34.200 --> 00:31:38.220
having a successful career and also having
a life of meaning.

00:31:38.220 --> 00:31:41.110
And that's a question I think a lot of us
in the audience are trying to

00:31:41.110 --> 00:31:44.750
wrestle with right now is, how do we have
both of those things?

00:31:44.750 --> 00:31:47.640
What advice do you have to share with us
about how we can think about our

00:31:47.640 --> 00:31:49.300
careers going forward?

00:31:52.280 --> 00:31:56.510
&gt;&gt; Oh, it's it, it, one of the aspects of
my life that we

00:31:56.510 --> 00:32:01.080
haven't talked about that's the most
special to me is, I'm a cancer doctor.

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:06.640
So as a cancer doctor, you interact with
people in a really special way.

00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:10.560
And that is often you have to tell people
that they're gonna die,

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:15.500
faster than you hope, and faster than they
would wish for themselves.

00:32:15.500 --> 00:32:19.210
And I think in the, in the worst ways, and

00:32:19.210 --> 00:32:21.390
the worst days that made me feel like a
failure.

00:32:21.390 --> 00:32:25.520
Like I just couldn't fix things that I
wanted to fix.

00:32:25.520 --> 00:32:27.390
And the best outcome of that for

00:32:27.390 --> 00:32:30.864
me was what you talked about before my own
sense of carpe diem.

00:32:30.864 --> 00:32:33.240
You know, every day's precious.

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:36.600
Every week is a great week that I get to
have.

00:32:36.600 --> 00:32:39.690
And so, I just really believe in having
fun.

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:44.100
If I'm in the middle of something hard, if
I'm working, if I'm not at work,

00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:49.460
I want everything I experience to be
meaningful and special.

00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:53.030
And I've not always been great at
work-life balance, but

00:32:53.030 --> 00:32:54.605
I'm really good at having fun.

00:32:54.605 --> 00:32:58.030
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I don't believe in suffering.

00:32:58.030 --> 00:32:59.630
It just is overrated.

00:33:00.750 --> 00:33:05.290
And I often tell people and in this, in
academia I can you guys,

00:33:05.290 --> 00:33:08.470
no dismissal of academia, but this thing
of being the most pitiful.

00:33:09.720 --> 00:33:13.650
I told faculty members, do not come in my
office and claim to be the most pitiful.

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:15.540
I'm not giving an award for that.

00:33:15.540 --> 00:33:18.450
You know, I don't believe in it.

00:33:18.450 --> 00:33:24.200
So, for me, and everybody's different, for
me, making sure that I have fun.

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:26.550
That I get to enjoy my colleagues at work.

00:33:26.550 --> 00:33:28.670
I spend a lot of time at work.

00:33:28.670 --> 00:33:30.060
I should enjoy my colleagues.

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:33.660
I should find out what about them is
interesting.

00:33:33.660 --> 00:33:37.290
That I can learn from and I can enjoy.

00:33:37.290 --> 00:33:38.950
I really like sports.

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:40.920
I really enjoy being with my husband.

00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:43.900
And I like my family and spend time with
my family.

00:33:43.900 --> 00:33:47.760
But just deciding that life's gonna be
fun.

00:33:47.760 --> 00:33:50.057
And that if there's every a week or

00:33:50.057 --> 00:33:54.426
a month where it's not, that it starts
with me making sure it is.

00:33:54.426 --> 00:33:58.434
And I tell my colleagues at the Gates
Foundation, cuz we're all into it and

00:33:58.434 --> 00:33:59.230
we over-do.

00:33:59.230 --> 00:34:02.310
We are serial over-doers.

00:34:02.310 --> 00:34:03.630
It's self manage.

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:08.450
Nobody's gonna understand as well as you
do how much sleep you need,

00:34:08.450 --> 00:34:10.900
how much fun you need, how much exercise
you need.

00:34:10.900 --> 00:34:14.110
What you should eat, who you should
surround yourself with.

00:34:14.110 --> 00:34:16.700
So manage yourself so that you can be
happy.

00:34:17.760 --> 00:34:20.710
And I think if there's any one thing that
I've tried to do, and

00:34:20.710 --> 00:34:22.620
I'm not always successful in doing it.

00:34:22.620 --> 00:34:29.250
Is to manage myself, so that I enjoy life,
and I can show up better if I do that.

00:34:30.390 --> 00:34:35.375
And that's been really good self-advice,
and I'm trying to take it.

00:34:35.375 --> 00:34:37.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:34:37.100 --> 00:34:38.370
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you, Sue.

00:34:38.370 --> 00:34:42.030
So, we're going to now, turn things over
to the audience for Q and A.

00:34:42.030 --> 00:34:45.870
We've got some mics that are gonna be
passed around on this first level.

00:34:45.870 --> 00:34:49.410
If you could raise your hand and keep your
hand raised.

00:34:49.410 --> 00:34:53.580
Once you get the mic please stand up and
share your name and

00:34:53.580 --> 00:34:56.180
affiliation before stating your question.

00:34:56.180 --> 00:35:00.440
We also, have, hashtag that

00:35:00.440 --> 00:35:04.350
you can use in order to tweet your
questions, which we'll also be taking.

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:07.500
That's a good option for those of you who
are in the balcony.

00:35:07.500 --> 00:35:11.570
So that hashtag is #GSBBFTT.

00:35:11.570 --> 00:35:14.780
And we'll start with a question over here,
please.

00:35:14.780 --> 00:35:15.752
&gt;&gt; Well, thank you so much.

00:35:15.752 --> 00:35:18.030
Marcello Palazzi, I'm a DCI Fellow here
and

00:35:18.030 --> 00:35:21.880
founder of the B Corp, which is a group of
progressive companies around the world.

00:35:21.880 --> 00:35:23.580
We're big fans of your foundation.

00:35:23.580 --> 00:35:25.190
Actually, one of the foundation I'm
involved,

00:35:25.190 --> 00:35:29.150
Talbot Foundation, gave an award to
Melinda Foundation last year.

00:35:29.150 --> 00:35:31.480
So well, I have a question.

00:35:31.480 --> 00:35:36.080
We in Europe, we see your foundation as a
kinda model of what, a U.S.

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:37.329
foundation can be.

00:35:37.329 --> 00:35:40.985
And sometimes, the question is, you seem
to be very focused on health, and

00:35:40.985 --> 00:35:41.967
that, and health.

00:35:41.967 --> 00:35:44.938
And given that many of the challenges in
the world are systemic,

00:35:44.938 --> 00:35:48.780
we just wonder whether you have
discussion, you have discussion about.

00:35:48.780 --> 00:35:53.187
This sorta dilemma being too focused
versus being more of a generalist.

00:35:53.187 --> 00:35:57.027
Because actually that's too traditional in
many U.S. foundations.

00:35:57.027 --> 00:35:57.820
Thank you.

00:35:57.820 --> 00:36:00.074
&gt;&gt; Thanks for the questions, so, so

00:36:00.074 --> 00:36:05.223
the foundation has, has a focus on global
health, global development.

00:36:05.223 --> 00:36:09.370
That includes agriculture, financial
services for the poor,.

00:36:09.370 --> 00:36:13.990
Some of the areas that are broader beyond
health, and in the U.S.

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:16.200
our primary focus is on U.S. education.

00:36:17.650 --> 00:36:22.190
The foundation is always asking ourselves
about the scope of our work, and

00:36:22.190 --> 00:36:25.110
where we spend our time and energy and our
funds.

00:36:25.110 --> 00:36:29.730
And we often have small projects that are
going on to access whether we

00:36:29.730 --> 00:36:33.400
should go into other areas and, and more
broad.

00:36:33.400 --> 00:36:38.040
One of the really positive aspects about
the focus on global health as an example.

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:42.000
And I think why the Gates Foundation
originally went down that

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:46.100
road is it's really clear what the global
burden of disease is.

00:36:46.100 --> 00:36:50.908
And one can make very crisp decisions to
use global burden of disease as

00:36:50.908 --> 00:36:53.938
your metric for what you're gonna tackle.

00:36:53.938 --> 00:36:55.106
There's a clarity of that.

00:36:55.106 --> 00:36:57.208
And a really clear purpose of doing that.

00:36:57.208 --> 00:37:01.990
So that we can have metrics both for
success and about what we tackle.

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:04.030
And when we look in some other areas,

00:37:04.030 --> 00:37:08.390
we always ask does the foundation have a
unique opportunity to contribute?

00:37:08.390 --> 00:37:13.730
And will we be able to measure and know if
our efforts are making a difference?

00:37:13.730 --> 00:37:16.670
Some of the most important things we're
asked about don't have those

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:19.890
attributes that are as easy to track or
measure.

00:37:19.890 --> 00:37:23.029
And yet, we continue to push ourselves to
ask that question.

00:37:25.480 --> 00:37:27.430
We have a question from Twitter?

00:37:27.430 --> 00:37:31.209
&gt;&gt; Yep this is from first year MBA Daniel
Burroughs.

00:37:31.209 --> 00:37:35.237
He you say the Gates Foundation in the
world, but

00:37:35.237 --> 00:37:37.549
is it big enough for objectives?

00:37:37.549 --> 00:37:44.222
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:37:44.222 --> 00:37:46.240
&gt;&gt; Yes, if.

00:37:46.240 --> 00:37:48.394
So we're big enough to meet our
objectives.

00:37:48.394 --> 00:37:53.705
If we think of our funds and our resources
which include

00:37:53.705 --> 00:37:59.240
the people's time and energy at the
foundation as leverage.

00:38:00.250 --> 00:38:04.100
And so, it's not uncommon for the
foundation to be involved in an effort.

00:38:05.130 --> 00:38:10.260
the, Global Alliance For Vaccines
Initiative is a good example.

00:38:10.260 --> 00:38:14.850
That are going to raise more than $7
billion to bring vaccines to

00:38:14.850 --> 00:38:16.440
the world's poorest people.

00:38:16.440 --> 00:38:21.260
That involves a global effort on global
vaccination.

00:38:21.260 --> 00:38:24.070
On low price accessible vaccines.

00:38:24.070 --> 00:38:29.130
Our efforts are magnified by the leverage
we get when others come in.

00:38:29.130 --> 00:38:32.310
So I think our ambitions are not too
large.

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:36.707
If we think of our efforts as bringing
others in and

00:38:36.707 --> 00:38:42.212
the leverage you get from the foundations
investments.

00:38:42.212 --> 00:38:45.711
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry, do you have the mic?

00:38:45.711 --> 00:38:47.136
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name's Austin.

00:38:47.136 --> 00:38:49.538
I had a question that kinda of
piggybacking off the,

00:38:49.538 --> 00:38:51.623
the response you gave to the first
question.

00:38:51.623 --> 00:38:54.946
When you said that there are a lot of
issues that are very difficult to

00:38:54.946 --> 00:38:56.050
measure outcomes for.

00:38:56.050 --> 00:38:58.720
And you're grappling with, kinda how to
address that.

00:38:58.720 --> 00:39:04.740
I was curious as to your thoughts on, do
you think that all beneficial or

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:07.930
positive outcomes that you're looking for
are eventually measurable?

00:39:07.930 --> 00:39:13.689
And if not, how do you think about making
change in arenas where

00:39:14.690 --> 00:39:18.980
the outcome we're looking for may never be
fully and accurately measured?

00:39:21.620 --> 00:39:25.670
&gt;&gt; That's, it, it, the direct answer to
your question is no.

00:39:25.670 --> 00:39:28.990
I think there are important things we care
about and

00:39:28.990 --> 00:39:31.040
should care about that can't be measured.

00:39:32.120 --> 00:39:35.680
And that, that brings a dilemma.

00:39:37.195 --> 00:39:42.230
It is one of the things that I think is
important to recognize if you're in

00:39:42.230 --> 00:39:47.030
a not for profit world is you have to do
better than good intentions.

00:39:47.030 --> 00:39:49.400
Right?
So good intentions don't change the world,

00:39:49.400 --> 00:39:50.850
by themselves.

00:39:50.850 --> 00:39:54.976
So I would be open to thinking about
something that can't have really clear,

00:39:54.976 --> 00:39:56.254
measurable outcomes.

00:39:56.254 --> 00:39:58.714
Like number of kids vaccinated, number of,

00:39:58.714 --> 00:40:03.260
of people who's lives were saved because
they didn't get measles, for example.

00:40:03.260 --> 00:40:04.130
Those are really.

00:40:04.130 --> 00:40:06.300
You know as a physician, I, I got that.

00:40:06.300 --> 00:40:07.060
Right?

00:40:07.060 --> 00:40:08.610
Poverty.

00:40:08.610 --> 00:40:11.300
Poverty's measurable, how much money a
family has,

00:40:11.300 --> 00:40:12.780
poverty's a measurable outcome.

00:40:12.780 --> 00:40:16.990
If you start to focus on poverty, you have
to look long term.

00:40:16.990 --> 00:40:19.960
But there are some metrics that can be
associated with that.

00:40:21.530 --> 00:40:24.880
Social justice is another thing that can
start to get into things.

00:40:24.880 --> 00:40:27.610
How do you know that you've got social
justice?

00:40:27.610 --> 00:40:30.780
What would be most important to me is that
there's some way to

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:33.519
know your investments are moving some
needle.

00:40:34.610 --> 00:40:35.785
So I think that there,

00:40:35.785 --> 00:40:41.610
there are maybe metrics that could be used
that can start to get at it.

00:40:41.610 --> 00:40:46.780
And get a sense for if you're changing
outcomes that you care about.

00:40:46.780 --> 00:40:47.332
but, but

00:40:47.332 --> 00:40:52.120
what would make me unhappy is if we got
into an area where we really had no clue.

00:40:52.120 --> 00:40:54.490
I just don't see us doing that.

00:40:54.490 --> 00:40:59.490
Because the chance to fritter away capital
that could be used on something where we

00:40:59.490 --> 00:41:01.540
can measure outcomes, would just be too
high.

00:41:03.910 --> 00:41:05.657
&gt;&gt; Do we have a question from this side?

00:41:05.657 --> 00:41:12.912
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:41:12.912 --> 00:41:16.067
&gt;&gt; In speaking with, your predecessor of
the Gates Foundation, Jeff Raikes.

00:41:16.067 --> 00:41:21.375
He mentioned a wide variety of people who
work in, in the foundation worlds.

00:41:21.375 --> 00:41:24.430
Sorta nonprofit types or who are
passionate about social justice,

00:41:24.430 --> 00:41:26.990
academics, who want to learn how to
measure impact.

00:41:26.990 --> 00:41:28.600
You know, with a lot of precision and

00:41:28.600 --> 00:41:30.940
private sector types who kinda just wanna
get things done.

00:41:30.940 --> 00:41:35.440
And all, all three of those types seems to
be part of your career background.

00:41:35.440 --> 00:41:37.230
So I guess my question is, you know,

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:40.730
what do you think you bring most to your
job leading the foundation?

00:41:40.730 --> 00:41:42.690
And do you think the balance is right?

00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:45.100
Do you think that those disparate voices
are leading to truth?

00:41:45.100 --> 00:41:47.060
Or are you trying to steer the foundation
in,

00:41:47.060 --> 00:41:48.959
in one of those directions more than the
others?

00:41:50.790 --> 00:41:54.460
&gt;&gt; I, I think it's great to have people
involved in the foundation world who

00:41:54.460 --> 00:41:56.740
come from a variety of backgrounds and
sectors.

00:41:56.740 --> 00:41:59.060
I think that's helpful and, and healthy.

00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:06.027
what, I, I, and I do think the mix
generates good outcomes.

00:42:06.027 --> 00:42:08.693
what, what I would focus on is,

00:42:08.693 --> 00:42:13.000
is a couple of things that I think need to
be balanced.

00:42:13.000 --> 00:42:16.910
What we've seen in the philanthropic world
is a lot of people who've

00:42:16.910 --> 00:42:20.560
been successful in the private sector come
into the not-for-profit world.

00:42:20.560 --> 00:42:22.900
And there's been a little bit of a culture
clash.

00:42:22.900 --> 00:42:26.970
And that's why I say this, this question
of, we know good when we see it,

00:42:26.970 --> 00:42:28.490
and good intentions.

00:42:28.490 --> 00:42:31.530
Have been sorta traditional in the
not-for-profit sector.

00:42:31.530 --> 00:42:36.620
Or were under funded and under salaried
and under a lot of things, so

00:42:36.620 --> 00:42:38.560
don't expect too much.

00:42:38.560 --> 00:42:41.980
That it can feel like that in the
not-for-profit sector.

00:42:41.980 --> 00:42:46.410
And so, I think bringing accountability,
resources, talent.

00:42:46.410 --> 00:42:49.080
Upping the ante is a really healthy thing.

00:42:50.170 --> 00:42:53.710
On the other hand I think it's a valid
criticism of a lot of

00:42:53.710 --> 00:42:55.320
newcomers to philanthropy.

00:42:55.320 --> 00:42:58.775
And I put myself on that list, that we can
bring a certain naivete.

00:42:58.775 --> 00:43:02.825
And the naivete is bringing western
solutions, or

00:43:02.825 --> 00:43:07.545
a western way of thinking, or a very techy
way of thinking.

00:43:07.545 --> 00:43:11.130
To problems that would be solved if we
actually sat down with the woman in

00:43:11.130 --> 00:43:14.252
the village, and she could tell you why
she can't get water.

00:43:14.252 --> 00:43:18.080
Because of something simple that you tried
to fix with a complex solution.

00:43:19.250 --> 00:43:23.360
And so, I think that the, the, the
combination of bringing the,

00:43:23.360 --> 00:43:27.320
those resources, that talent, that
passion, that accountability.

00:43:27.320 --> 00:43:32.240
With, with the humility to understand that
on the ground in a different culture,

00:43:32.240 --> 00:43:33.810
you really don't know.

00:43:33.810 --> 00:43:35.470
And everything's a collaboration.

00:43:36.540 --> 00:43:41.750
I think that combination can generate
something that can be very positive.

00:43:41.750 --> 00:43:47.610
That and the last thing I'll say on that
is the as impatient as I am.

00:43:47.610 --> 00:43:51.112
I do think the most important great

00:43:51.112 --> 00:43:57.161
philanthropic outcomes are measured in
years not months.

00:43:57.161 --> 00:43:59.466
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
I'll take a question from Twitter but

00:43:59.466 --> 00:44:02.321
I also want to say, please make sure to
state your name and

00:44:02.321 --> 00:44:04.001
affiliation when we call on you.

00:44:04.001 --> 00:44:05.837
&gt;&gt; So this is from Sharon.

00:44:05.837 --> 00:44:09.727
She wants to know, you, you mentioned in
the beginning about ways to

00:44:09.727 --> 00:44:14.170
motivate people and get them to provide
clear answers and take risks.

00:44:14.170 --> 00:44:15.350
What kinda behaviors don't you reward?

00:44:15.350 --> 00:44:17.550
Or, or do you discourage?

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:23.530
&gt;&gt; Oh.
[LAUGH] I'm trying to, I'm trying to,

00:44:23.530 --> 00:44:26.455
to, to give a short a short answer.

00:44:26.455 --> 00:44:27.930
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:44:27.930 --> 00:44:35.190
&gt;&gt; Some of my least favorite probably my
very least favorite is, it wasn't me.

00:44:35.190 --> 00:44:37.320
That guy's the reason that failed.

00:44:37.320 --> 00:44:38.990
If not for him or his function or

00:44:38.990 --> 00:44:42.350
his people, we'd be in a different and
better place.

00:44:42.350 --> 00:44:49.545
That's a really high on my list of, of an
answer not to give.

00:44:49.545 --> 00:44:53.778
the, the other one is, is that we work
with a partner, and,

00:44:53.778 --> 00:44:58.592
you know, they saw it this way and that's
because they're stupid.

00:44:58.592 --> 00:45:00.215
Not because they disagreed with us, or

00:45:00.215 --> 00:45:03.422
they come at from a different point of
view, or have a different experience.

00:45:03.422 --> 00:45:07.648
Is it to, to underestimate either a
partner or a different function.

00:45:07.648 --> 00:45:09.060
I love team work and, and

00:45:09.060 --> 00:45:13.637
one of the great things I was thrown into
when I worked at Bristol-Myers Squibb,

00:45:13.637 --> 00:45:17.876
is I got on a team when I worked on Taxol
with Manufacturing, and Sales, and

00:45:17.876 --> 00:45:21.760
Regulatory and, and all these people who
had no idea what they did.

00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:25.810
But I came really quickly to understand
none of the great stuff I wanted to

00:45:25.810 --> 00:45:27.980
have happen would happen without them.

00:45:27.980 --> 00:45:30.871
And so the fact that I didn't know what
Sales and

00:45:30.871 --> 00:45:34.173
Marketing did was me being ignorant not
them having value.

00:45:34.173 --> 00:45:38.537
[LAUGH] And, and I think that's, that's
the very important thing, particularly for

00:45:38.537 --> 00:45:40.990
technical people, scientists, physicians,

00:45:40.990 --> 00:45:44.633
is not to over estimate the fact that
because you know science, or IT, or

00:45:44.633 --> 00:45:48.510
something that you, that extends into
knowing everything.

00:45:48.510 --> 00:45:50.391
So that would be on the list too [LAUGH].

00:45:50.391 --> 00:45:53.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Do we have a question from
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:45:53.290 --> 00:45:55.959
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:45:55.959 --> 00:45:57.538
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Roz Naylor.

00:45:57.538 --> 00:46:01.105
I direct the Center on Food Security and
the Environment at Stanford and

00:46:01.105 --> 00:46:03.980
a professor of Environmental Earth System
and Science.

00:46:03.980 --> 00:46:08.330
I really enjoyed the interview on both
your parts, it's really wonderful.

00:46:08.330 --> 00:46:12.440
I had a questions about coming into a
large organization where you

00:46:12.440 --> 00:46:17.030
have an incredible Global Health Program,
Agricultural Development Program,

00:46:17.030 --> 00:46:18.730
Education Program.

00:46:18.730 --> 00:46:21.937
And at one level you've got such talented
people you want to

00:46:21.937 --> 00:46:26.584
just give the best incentives and let
those divisions really take off.

00:46:26.584 --> 00:46:30.106
Yet at the intersection of those different
divisions might be

00:46:30.106 --> 00:46:31.874
the most creative solutions.

00:46:31.874 --> 00:46:36.692
For example, a number of the global health
issues may really be dependent on

00:46:36.692 --> 00:46:42.163
much better nutrition, or the ending of
malnutrition in those areas.

00:46:42.163 --> 00:46:46.847
similarly, the best advances in
agricultural development may be

00:46:46.847 --> 00:46:51.619
the solving of some of the productivity
depleting disease issues.

00:46:51.619 --> 00:46:58.140
How do you motivate really good creativity
and productivity at the intersections

00:46:58.140 --> 00:47:03.260
when, when you really need some
disciplinary strength as well?

00:47:03.260 --> 00:47:05.540
&gt;&gt; That is so hard to do.

00:47:05.540 --> 00:47:07.930
I can tell you that the, it's one of the
biggest challenges.

00:47:07.930 --> 00:47:10.900
I'll give you two ways that I've started
to think about this.

00:47:12.080 --> 00:47:13.097
One is geography.

00:47:13.097 --> 00:47:17.651
So, w, we can take advantage of the fact
that we have multiple programs going on in

00:47:17.651 --> 00:47:21.810
certain geographies, like Northern India,
like Ethiopia.

00:47:21.810 --> 00:47:26.020
And we have what's called a tech panel,
where we bring together in the geography,

00:47:26.020 --> 00:47:29.040
all of, the various silos in the
foundation,

00:47:29.040 --> 00:47:32.210
who are working on different aspects of
problems.

00:47:32.210 --> 00:47:38.140
So it may be nutrition, family planning
vaccination and agriculture.

00:47:38.140 --> 00:47:40.000
But all being done in Ethiopia.

00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:44.540
So bringing together those folks so that
they know what each other are doing.

00:47:44.540 --> 00:47:47.090
And coordinate through our country offices
and

00:47:47.090 --> 00:47:52.230
through that geography can start to help
us connect those dots and look across.

00:47:52.230 --> 00:47:55.364
The other thing that I'm trying to do at
the foundation is

00:47:55.364 --> 00:47:57.986
have a very strong executive leadership
team.

00:47:57.986 --> 00:48:01.173
If we can't expect people in the trenches
to collaborate,

00:48:01.173 --> 00:48:04.488
if the executive leadership team isn't
collaborating, and

00:48:04.488 --> 00:48:08.567
I think in the foundation, our foundation,
we've expected the executive

00:48:08.567 --> 00:48:12.478
leadership team to focus a lot of business
process and less on strategy.

00:48:12.478 --> 00:48:16.647
I'm trying to turn that on its head, and
have a much more strategic focus on

00:48:16.647 --> 00:48:20.603
the executive leadership team and look at
those intersections.

00:48:20.603 --> 00:48:25.928
Including the executive leadership team
showing up in pairs, in geographies, and

00:48:25.928 --> 00:48:29.753
at the foundation, so we can start
connecting at our level.

00:48:29.753 --> 00:48:33.143
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:48:33.143 --> 00:48:35.164
&gt;&gt; Oh, we'll take this question little.

00:48:35.164 --> 00:48:39.225
&gt;&gt; Good afternoon I'm an MBA1 student to
continue somewhat with

00:48:39.225 --> 00:48:41.397
Professor Nailer's question.

00:48:41.397 --> 00:48:44.441
How do you balance the funding the funding
of

00:48:44.441 --> 00:48:49.940
new technology versus the implementation
of a specific technology on a sector?

00:48:49.940 --> 00:48:55.620
And then assuming that, that varies by, by
sector does this have implications for

00:48:55.620 --> 00:48:57.910
how businesses should consider
opportunities?

00:49:00.970 --> 00:49:04.110
&gt;&gt; There's, there's not a neat and tidy
answer to your question.

00:49:04.110 --> 00:49:09.170
So, so, I, I, I will tell you that the the
way I

00:49:09.170 --> 00:49:14.260
start to think about this is there are
times when delivery as you

00:49:14.260 --> 00:49:19.920
call it there sort of making existing
tools reach where they need to reach.

00:49:19.920 --> 00:49:22.254
Makes good sense.

00:49:22.254 --> 00:49:25.460
Often at scale, that's the job of the
Ministry of Health for example or

00:49:25.460 --> 00:49:27.240
the Ministry of Agriculture.

00:49:27.240 --> 00:49:33.200
So, so for me the delivery aspect of what
we do has to be very clear,

00:49:33.200 --> 00:49:36.740
often involving innovation, or a pilot, or

00:49:36.740 --> 00:49:39.090
trying to do something that then can be
scaled.

00:49:39.090 --> 00:49:42.320
Because we're never gonna deliver on a
global basis.

00:49:42.320 --> 00:49:44.670
that's, that's for others to do that.

00:49:44.670 --> 00:49:48.700
So we have to be extremely purposeful with
our delivery methods, and, and

00:49:48.700 --> 00:49:53.380
be thoughtful about what that looks like,
and how we make that real.

00:49:53.380 --> 00:49:56.730
So that often involves delivery
innovation.

00:49:56.730 --> 00:50:00.920
So really important that delivery
innovation is a part of what we do.

00:50:00.920 --> 00:50:03.330
On the technology side we, we have an
affinity, and

00:50:03.330 --> 00:50:06.240
I have an affinity for technology
solutions.

00:50:06.240 --> 00:50:10.120
But I look at technology solutions through
a really clear lens.

00:50:10.120 --> 00:50:14.110
And that is, is, will this really change
things?

00:50:14.110 --> 00:50:19.010
So I was involved in a discussion
yesterday about cervical cancer.

00:50:19.010 --> 00:50:20.510
Screening for cervical cancer and

00:50:20.510 --> 00:50:23.750
preventing cervical cancer is really hard
at scale.

00:50:23.750 --> 00:50:25.640
It involves women who are 35 to 50,

00:50:25.640 --> 00:50:31.650
a little older than typical use the health
care system in a low resource country.

00:50:31.650 --> 00:50:36.360
And you have to see many, many women
before you can truly prevent cancer.

00:50:36.360 --> 00:50:43.060
So, if we had a diagnostic that looks at
Oncogenic Papillovi, Papilloma Virus.

00:50:43.060 --> 00:50:46.870
If, if we had something that really was,
was transformative in

00:50:46.870 --> 00:50:53.070
saying all these women don't need this
expensive hard to execute screening.

00:50:53.070 --> 00:50:55.290
That would be a transformative diagnostic.

00:50:55.290 --> 00:50:57.026
That's the kind of thing I wanna find.

00:50:57.026 --> 00:51:01.305
So often the best discussions I'm involved
in talk about delivery,

00:51:01.305 --> 00:51:04.919
talk about a missing technology and the
likelihood that we

00:51:04.919 --> 00:51:09.641
can provide something that transforms that
delivery, that the ministry,

00:51:09.641 --> 00:51:14.910
government, others are providing because
everything's easier after that.

00:51:14.910 --> 00:51:16.379
That's where I think we're at our finest.

00:51:18.410 --> 00:51:23.390
&gt;&gt; Given the limited time remaining I want
to conclude with a question, Sue,

00:51:23.390 --> 00:51:26.470
that is near and dear to many of us in the
audience.

00:51:26.470 --> 00:51:28.160
What matters most to you and why?

00:51:28.160 --> 00:51:30.924
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:51:30.924 --> 00:51:34.860
&gt;&gt; What matters most to me is that the,

00:51:34.860 --> 00:51:41.291
as a leader I can allow for really
talented people

00:51:41.291 --> 00:51:48.400
to do things that improve people's chance
at a happy life.

00:51:50.830 --> 00:51:54.890
The reason for me is, and it kinda gets
back to how I was raised and

00:51:54.890 --> 00:51:55.960
the environment I was in.

00:51:55.960 --> 00:52:02.280
Just, I became a physician because I
really wanted, and want to think that,

00:52:04.110 --> 00:52:09.470
you know, to, to the, The Gates Foundation
motto is all lives have equal value.

00:52:09.470 --> 00:52:13.360
So that everybody has a chance to be
healthy and happy and

00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:17.460
have a, the life they'd like for
themselves and their families.

00:52:17.460 --> 00:52:23.860
And so thinking about how you know, when I
was a doctor and interacted with a patient

00:52:23.860 --> 00:52:28.950
one at a time, I cared so deeply that that
patient would have a good experience.

00:52:28.950 --> 00:52:32.895
And when I left the general practice of
medicine and moved as a leader.

00:52:32.895 --> 00:52:36.796
My hope is that many more patients and
many more people.

00:52:36.796 --> 00:52:39.342
&gt;&gt; [COUGH].
&gt;&gt; Will have a chance to have the kind of

00:52:39.342 --> 00:52:41.550
life I hope for them.

00:52:41.550 --> 00:52:46.332
And that the really talented people I get
to work with are inspired and

00:52:46.332 --> 00:52:51.961
have that passion and oomph and drive to
create those things, and that I helped.

00:52:51.961 --> 00:52:54.957
&gt;&gt; Well thank you so much for your
insights and your candor.

00:52:54.957 --> 00:52:58.299
And we can't wait to see how you continue
to improve the world.

00:52:58.299 --> 00:52:59.228
So thank you so much, Sue.

00:52:59.228 --> 00:53:03.850
[APPLAUSE].

00:53:03.850 --> 00:53:09.260
[MUSIC]

